e to learn  languages in other words to become  linguists  because they have a tendency to  complicate things  introduce complicated terms whether it  be terms of grammar or terms like  phonemes and morphemes and i don't know  what other kind of themes  so i don't think you need to be a  linguistics expert you don't even need  to be an expert in applied linguistics  to offer advice on language learning if  you have learned a number of languages i  think you have useful  and potentially valuable experience to  share with other people people can form  their own opinions  and they may be stimulated by what you  say or not stimulated or turned off by  what you say  so that  to clarify a linguist is someone who  speaks more than one language  i believe that  many many more people in the world could  be linguists because it's made too  complicated now in that regard  the article  by professor bloom is very interesting  because  it's not just today that we feel that  language teaching has been made too  complicated  this was an opinion expressed by john  locke the british philosopher in the  17th century  there's another mr hamilton i can't  remember his name but you'll see the  details in the article by professor  bloom who points out that  you know in the middle ages people learn  languages by reading  and they had interlinear translations  and you know abundant glossaries to help  them through these texts and by reading  subjects of interest  they acquired the language and that they  acquired it more quickly that way than  uh in subsequent centuries when the  emphasis was more on on grammar and  instruction  so that uh it's an interesting and also  this article about professor bloom  points out justin how important  vocabulary is  that while a small number of words may  account for 70 or 80 percent  of the uh  of most contexts  in fact the remaining 20  consists of a lot of words including a  lot of relatively rare words so that to  participate in an intelligent  conversation beyond just you know  ordering beer  requires a lot of words and of course  all of that is behind the way we have  structured link we have sort of a modern  equivalent of these glossaries  in the way our little link widget works  which we're constantly improving  now you don't even have to go to the  dictionary the meaning slots in right  away  uh with our flashcard being built in and  we're expanding our notes section to  each lesson so that  because the objective i believe as a  language learner is to get to real  authentic meaningful texts as soon as  possible again christoph plexton  criticizes me for not realizing he  believes i don't realize  that um  krashen was  promoted this idea of comprehensible  input  um you know i know that but  i think that we can help make input more  comprehensible and if you can combine  uh comprehensible input  with making it interesting then that's  going to be much more  uh effective for the learner because  you can't start with comprehensible  input you know at first the  comprehensive  comprehensible portion isn't 95 percent  it's zero percent and you work your way  up from that and if you have the audio  and if you have the glossary or the  kinds of uh functionality that we have  at link you can work your way through  these texts and i remember doing it  within three months of being in russian  i was attacking russian novels because  that's what interested me  so  yes again cluckston refers to total  physical response and other teaching  methods which are fine there's nothing  wrong with these they're probably quite  effective but it requires a classroom  for tpr whereas all i'm saying is and  again benny the irish polyglot is always  in favor of charging off to the country  and speaking to people and corralling  people to talk to and stuff and in  certain circumstances that might work  it's not something that i would choose  to do  but all i'm saying is there is this  other  tremendously easy  approach which is to focus on input and  to build up your vocabulary so that you  can understand what people are talking  about so that when you do have an  opportunity to speak you're going to do  better but the big advantage of the  input based approach  is not only that it was effective as you  will see if you read  this article about professor bloom it  was effective way back in the middle  ages  but it's all under control you don't  rely on the good will or indulgence of  some native speaker has to talk to you  while you try to babble  you can decide what you're going to  listen to  and nowadays with mp3 players with the  internet with all these wonderful things  it becomes easier and easier much easier  than it was in the middle ages to access  interesting content  uh  just a few other things and with  reference to the uh  to the clunkston video  he sort of implies and benny does the  same that uh i'm only interested in  reading and i'm not interested in  speaking but that's not true and people  shouldn't like shouldn't ascribe motives  to people when they don't know uh to me  input based learning is the fastest way  to get to effective communication  uh to benny listening to mp3 players and  reading is inhuman or anti-social or  something okay he doesn't like to read  that's fine  i think reading  is is  tremendously powerful in your own  language and in learning other languages  we have a disagreement but don't assume  that i don't like to speak and similarly  clarkston uh  you know says oh moses mccormick speaks  all these languages but he never has a  chance to speak them  benny lives in europe so he's actually  learning languages that he can speak  well  in fact  you know when we talk about the joy of  being a linguist listen to a few of  moses's  videos tell me if he doesn't enjoy  speaking those languages  tell me if he doesn't enjoy meeting some  an estonian or someone from i don't know  cambodia or kenya i don't know all the  languages that he's learned  he enjoys doing that and and why is that  not a good thing why can't how can you  criticize someone for doing that and i'm  quite sure  that that moses  knows that his 15th and 20th language  better  than benny knows his czech and hungarian  or his tie where he went amidst great  fanfare and and claimed that within two  months he was going to learn that  language so  to each his own i mean  i'm not saying that benny didn't pick up  anything but  to criticize people who have a somewhat  more systematic approach  i think is unjustified and  we are all going to follow the the  method that we enjoy the most because  ultimately that's the key is to enjoy it  and so to summarize here a linguist is  someone who speaks more than one  language  there's a tremendous amount of of  enjoyment and joy associated with being  a linguist and  consuming massive amounts of input not  only 95 comprehensible  is very effective and it has been sold  historically and hopefully now with  internet and mp3 technology it will be  again  thank you for listening
 

